; 21-case.kurt

; `case` is `assume` with case distinctions in mind: several `case` blocks in
; a row, each assuming one alternative, combine into a case distinction --
; if you can reach the same goal from every case of a disjunction, you've
; proved the goal outright.  Kurt logs that step as `case-elim`.
;
; For that to work, the disjunction itself (`A or B` below, `use`d up front)
; must already be known -- `case` does NOT check it, or that your cases are
; exhaustive at all; without it the goal just fails with an ordinary "can not
; derive", not a `case`-specific error.
; Restating the goal after the case blocks (the `C` before `qed` below) is
; optional: without it, `qed` derives the goal by `case-elim` itself.
; The loaded propositional theory's general binary `or-elim` rule gives `or` this meaning; the
; spelling `or` by itself does not.

load prop
bool A, B, C
use A or B
use A implies C
use B implies C

show C
proof
    case A
        C
    case B
        C
    C
qed
